{
  "verdict": "✅ Correct answer: (a) まもなく\nThis word means \"soon\" and fits an announcement that the train is approaching.",
  "why": [
    "✓ (a) まもなく: correct because \"soon\" fits a context announcing something that will happen shortly.",
    "✗ (b) ちかぢか: incorrect because it means \"recently, just recently\" and is used for actions that have already occurred.",
    "✗ (c) すでに: incorrect because it means \"already\" and refers to something completed.",
    "✗ (d) さっそく: incorrect because it means \"immediately, right away\" and usually accompanies an action performed at once."
  ],
  "explanation": "「まもなく」is an adverb of time used to describe something that will happen very soon, with the nuance of an approaching event. In the sentence \"（　　）３番線に東京行きの電車がまいります\", a word indicating the near future is needed, so まもなく is correct. The complete sentence means: \"The Tokyo-bound train will arrive at Track 3 shortly.\" Example: \"まもなく雨が降り始めます。\" (It will start raining soon.) To distinguish the choices, ちかぢか means \"recently, just recently\" for an event that has happened, すでに means \"already\" for a completed event, and さっそく means \"immediately\" and usually precedes an action carried out at once."
}
